course: dictionary
lesson: 7445
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The same word can name a person, a ball of wool or a dog. The clues around the word show you which meaning the writer means. «In Perth, Maddie gets called «Pom», Australian for an English person.» Perth and Australian are the clues, so Pom means an English person here.
explain: Pom is slang, so it is informal talk. In Australia and New Zealand people say «the Poms» for the English cricket or rugby team. It is often a joke, but it can also be unkind, so think about how someone would feel before you use it.
explain: Look at the other words to find the meaning. A craft lesson about wool means a pom-pom, a fluffy ball. A talk about a small, fluffy pet means a Pomeranian. Always ask: which meaning fits this sentence?
fact: A pom, or Pommy, is slang in Australia and New Zealand for an English or British person.
fact: «Pom» is often a joking word, but it can sound rude, so be careful how you use it.
fact: A pom is short for a pom-pom, a fluffy ball of wool or a cheerleader's bunch of ribbons.
fact: A pom is also short for a Pomeranian, a small fluffy dog.
fact: «The Poms» means the English team in sport, such as cricket or rugby.
exercise: «In Perth, Maddie gets called «Pom», Australian for an English person.» What does Pom mean here? | A small fluffy dog | A fluffy ball of wool | *An English person
exercise: Mia is making a woolly hat and needs a «pom» for the top. What is a pom? | A cricket bat | *A fluffy ball of wool | A Scottish tartan scarf | A small fluffy dog
exercise: Which word in «The Poms won the cricket match.» means the English team? | *Poms | won | cricket | match
test: Why should you be careful when you call someone a Pom? | Because it means a fluffy ball | Because it is a long word | Because it is always a compliment | *Because it can be teasing or unkind
test: «The cheerleaders waved their poms.» What are the poms? | *Bunches of ribbons | Small fluffy dogs | English people
exam: In «The Pomeranian chased a pom-pom across the room.», what is the pom-pom? | An English person | *A fluffy ball | A cricket team | A small fluffy dog
